.Dd October 22, 2026
.Dt FSCD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fscd
.Nd "service state monitoring daemon"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dfVv
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl p Ar pidfile
.Op Fl s Ar socket
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
.Fx
Service Control Daemon is a service monitor which also implements a
restart, restart retry, and log notification mechanism.
The
.Nm
utility monitors the states of other services
and will perform multiple attempts to restart them if they die prematurely.
.Pp
When invoked, the
.Nm
utility will read the list of pre-defined services to monitor
from its configuration file,
.Pa /usr/local/etc/fscd.conf ,
and create a socket to listen for messages from
.Xr fscadm 8 ,
.Fx
the services control administration utility.
The
.Xr fscadm 8
management utility will allow for the addition or deletion of services from the
.Nm
utility's internal monitoring list.
.Pp
When monitored services terminate,
.Xr fscd 8
will attempt to restart them and log status update messages using the
.Dv LOG_NOTICE
facility.
Services that fail this restart will be logged via the
.Dv LOG_ERR
facility.
If the
.Nm
utility is unable to successfully start a service, that service will be placed
inside of a retry queue.
The retry queue will make several attempts to perform the restart while
writing status updates, including retry count entries into
.Pa /var/log/messages
for review.
The retry queue is not infinite and uses a monotonic clock to set retry
times, refreshing these times as each service fails to start up the point
where either the service will restart or the retry count is exhausted.
.Pp
The
.Nm
utility uses the
.Xr kqueue 2
interface to receive notifications regarding process terminations.
The
.Nm
utility also utilizes the facilities provided through
.Xr service 8
for (re-)starting services, getting their PIDs and initially checking
on their current status and service state.
.Pp
The
.Nm
utility is subject the same rules which govern
.Xr rc 8 ;
meaning that services must have an entry in
.Pa /etc/rc.conf
before they may be managed by the
.Nm
utility.
After successfully reading the list of services from
.Pa /usr/local/etc/fscd.conf
and becoming a daemon, services may be added or removed
from monitoring through the use of the
.Xr fscadm 8
management utility.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.Ss SERVICE STATE
A service managed by the
.Nm
utility must be in one of the following states:
.Bl -tag -width "Unregistered"
.It Sy Disabled
The service is not running yet remains registered in the service list
and does not have a retry queue entry.
Disabled services are not automatically removed and require human intervention.
.It Sy Enabled
The service is registered in the service list, is currently running,
its PID(s) are known, and it is registered with the
.Xr kqueue 2
watcher for exit monitoring.
.It Sy Restarting
A restart operation is in progress and other workers must be blocked from
initiating a competing restart for that service.
.It Sy Pending
A restart attempt has failed and the service has at least one pending retry
scheduled for a future attempt.
The service may have stale PID entries or no PID entries at all.
.It Sy Unregistered
The service was removed from the monitored service list by a privileged user
and will not be restarted.
Any pending retry attempts should have also been removed.
Any stale entries, in the unlikely case they exist, will be removed when the
worker attempts to issue a restart.
.El
.Ss RESTART POLICY
Service management using the
.Nm
utility follows a basic restart policy that is, or should be, constrained
to the following:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Unexpected service exits trigger an immediate restart attempt after being
notified through the
.Xr kqueue 2
watcher.
.It
If a restart fails, the service is scheduled for a pre-defined
number of retry attempts.
Right now, this is hard coded at five (5) but in the future it may be
an option supplied on the command line or specified in the configuration file.
.It
Each retry has a deadline calculated using
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC .
.It
A successful restart has three requirements including the service successfully
started and is running, obtaining and updating the new PID assigned to the
service, and a successful registration with the
.Xr kqueue 2
watcher.
After which, any pending retry queue entries are removed.
.It
If PID discovery or
.Xr kqueue 2
registration fails, the service restart is considered failed even if the
service is up and running.
Either failure makes monitoring unreliable and a privileged user should
intervene and review the information available; possibly removing and
re-adding the service to monitoring.
.It
When another restart operation is in progress, there should be no attempt
from another worker to perform restarts.
Several checks are included to prevent possible retry loops and, once a
restart is successful, the retry entry should be removed.
.It
A service should never be removed except by a privileged user.
Once the initial restart fails, the service is registered with the
retry queue and remains there until either a successful restart occurs
or the retry attempts are exhausted.
After every retry attempt has failed, the service will enter the
disabled state.
.It
Services in a disabled state require intervention from a privileged user.
.It
Once a service has been unregistered, it should be removed from the
monitored service list, the
.Xr kqueue 2
watcher, and the retry queue.
No retry entry should re-register the service for monitoring.
.El
.Ss SIGNALS
Several signals do not restart the service immediately as they may be the
result of a privileged user taking action.
Instead, the
.Nm
utility will wait up to 60 seconds for the service to restart.
These are:
.Bl -dash -compact
.It
SIGINT
.It
SIGINT
.It
SIGTERM
.It
SIGUSR1
.It
SIGUSR2
.It
SIGKILL
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl d
Run
.Nm
in the background as a daemon.
The default is to run in the foreground and show service change messages
on the console.
.It Fl f
Force overwriting a pre-existing socket.
A stale socket may exist if the
.Nm
utility is already running or was not properly shut down previously.
.It Fl V
Print out the version of
.Nm
and exit.
.It Fl v
Verbose, run in the foreground and print messages to the console instead of
sending them to the
.Xr syslog 3
facility.
The
.Nm
utility has several verbosity levels to increase or decrease the amount
of notifications sent.
The default setting is
.Sq 1
which logs all messages assigned either the
.Dv LOG_ERR
or
.Dv LOG_NOTICE
will be available in the
.Pa /var/log/messages
log file.
.It Fl c Ar file
Use the alternative configuration file specified by
.Ar file
instead of the default
.Pa /usr/local/etc/fscd.conf .
.It Fl p Ar pidfile
Use the supplied, alternative
.Ar pidfile
instead of the standard file created at
.Pa /var/run/fscd.pid .
.It Fl s Ar socket
Use the alternative
.Ar socket
instead of the default socket created at
.Pa /var/run/fscd.sock .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/fscd.conf
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/fscd.conf
Name and location of the configuration file.
Pre-defined monitoring lists will be read from this file
during initialization.
.It Pa /var/run/fscd.pid
The default pidfile created when the
.Nm
utility is started.
.It Pa /var/run/fscd.sock
Name and location of the
.Ux
domain socket used to communicate information between
.Xr fscd 8
and
.Xr fscadm 8 .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr signal 3 ,
.Xr syslog 3 ,
.Xr fscd.conf 5 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr fscadm 8 ,
.Xr rc 8 ,
.Xr rcorder 8 ,
.Xr service 8
